ecause the variance of the data  we're looking at really depends on the  selected time frame so for instance if i  actually compare them or evaluate my  model only over a short amount of time  uh like this down here i only observe at  like a small variance in the target  variable because our data is highly  correlated also if i choose a larger  window i might get a larger variance in  the actual target variable and in fact  because we have a non-stationary process  it doesn't converge for infinite time  windows also we don't have infinite time  windows um so  if you evaluate models of a particular  time frame uh you have to be aware that  i can't compare model performances if i  normalize it by the target variants  over different time frames so if i  choose one year i have to stick with one  year and it doesn't make sense to  compare it with evaluations of half a  year or even shorter time scales but in  order to get rid of this  assumption or this problem of choosing a  time frame what you can always do is  compare your model performance to simple  bass lines so you should always use  simple bass lines and  a first natural choice of simple bass  line is always a linear model  still our nilia model has quite some  assumptions because we still need to do  pre-processing for the linear models so  that means we need to bake in  assumptions for scaling imputation and  of course  make a choice of features  and here's one advantage when you have  highly correlated data in time  there's a very easy way to come up with  a very simple baseline that has  basically no assumptions baked in and  that's just using a rolling median or a  rolling mean over a certain time frame  or the last x samples and then you have  a baseline that has no assumptions apart  from just the window size or the number  of samples  and if you can't even if your machine  learning even can't beat this rolling  media it's probably not worth applying  machine learning to it so that's a very  very nice base