after almost a month to figure out how  much compressive strength it can  withstand sorry here's now where machine  learning gets into the mix so we will  use machine learning to get or we do use  machine learning to get the cement  strength on point um so what we do is we  actually predict the cement strength  that would develop 28 days later right  at the time of production so we can  immediately counteract if something's  going wrong  so here's here's the principle so first  of all where do these fluctuations in  the compressive strength stem from well  cement is made out of a natural material  and that is limestone  and limestone might vary from cubic  meters to cubic meter even within the  same quarry so the chemical composition  and mineralogically can actually change  and these changes in the composition of  the limestone then yield changes and  fluctuations in the compressive strength  so what we want to do is basically just  counteract these compressive strength  changes by adjusting the fineness of the  material because the fineness of cement  is a good lever to influence the  compressor strength the more fine i  grind my cement and the more surface  area it has to go  to react with water the stronger it gets  so meaning if uh due to natural  fluctuation the cement gets stronger we  can actually grind more coarsely and if  it's getting not strong enough we can  grind more finer and with that  we then can achieve a material that has  a very reliable and homogeneous and  consistent strength over time  so  here's how our application actually  integrates into a cement plant what you  see here is a depiction of the last step  of the cement production the cement mill  so we are now right after the kiln  remember kiln produces clinker heavy co2  footprint but here are the other  supplementary cementitious materials  like fly ash slag and the carbon free  fine limestone so all so these get mixed  together according to your cement recipe  chucked into the cement mill to actually  grind them very very finely and then  there's this filter or separator step  that can separate fine particles from  both ones so the fines ones are finished  and then the coarse ones have to take  extra turns through the cement mill and  by influencing the separator and some  settings of the cement mold we can  actually influence the fineness of the  material but it doesn't stop there so in  a cement plant there are regularly  cement samples taken right after the  cement mill and these are automatically  analyzed with x-ray fluorescence x-ray  diffrectomy and a particle size analyzer  so um this gives us a characterization  of the  samples in terms of its chemical  composition crystal structure and actual  distributions of particles and this data  then is exported into our cloud ai  system  where we then run machine learning on  top of it to really predict the  compressive strength that would have  developed or will developed 28 days  later and now we can react immediately  and don't have to wait a month to figure  out how strong our stuff is going to get  so for instance if then we predict that  the cement is getting too strong we can  grind more coarsely if it's if you're  predicting it's getting not strong  enough we can grind more fine  actually what we do is simply compute  optimal set points for the cement mills  in order to achieve this and then these  set points are fed back into the plant  the cement is adjusted either by the  operator or directly by the expert  system in place and then when we draw a  sample two or three hours later we will  see okay we have uh reacted to the  changes and now the cement strength is  really on point again  and here's how that actually looks like  um in like how the results actually look  like that we achieved in the plant so  what you can see here our strength test  of a cement plant uh over about five  years and every blue dot here  corresponds to one particular strength  test where they actually formed such a  cube that i just showed in the video let  it sit and harden for 28 days and then  crush it  and as you can see um in this rolling  median function here that basically the  fluctuations are quite substantial over  time so  um the the fluctuations in the spend  strength go from like 50 to 65  megapascals and that's actually quite a  lot and now from here on as you can see  uh we applied our solution into the  plant um and we were able to reduce  these fluctuations by about 30 percent  um which then for an average cement  plants amounts to savings of around 300  to half a million euros and it's a  mixture of savings and energy savings in  co2 certificates and actually higher  profit margins due to better quality  so  basically this is the whole control loop  and the machine learning service that we  provide for cement  plans and we've deployed this now  in  four plans plans five and six are soon  to be coming but actually getting there  and having the whole system running was  quite a bumpy ride and we had some  learnings on the way and that i want to  share with you now so learning number  one or lesson number one is do not spend  too much time on complex models i mean  we tried many different machine learning  models and so because it's fun i mean we  tried pytorch neural networks i mean  deep learning is fun  you can play around with the network  architecture  you can play around with the learning  rate even the learning algorithm itself  test dropout you name it lots of stuff  to try out of course we try support  vector machines gaussian processes  xgboost you name it we tried it um  unfortunately all of these gave  basically no improvements even worse  they are quite black boxy models so what  we really ended up with in production is  a combination of regularized linear  regression and a random forest for the  non-linearities  that's actually great because just  looking at the coefficients of the  linear regression you can really  understand what the model learned from  the data and that's actually important  because we deploy our models in order to  really control cement life  meaning we have to be confident that the  models not only picked up some spurious  correlations between the features and  cement strength but actually learned  causal  causal relations that are also known to  smed engineers  so for instance of course it needs to  learn that fineness is a good level to  change strength because that's the one  we're optimizing with but if stuff pops  out that has been used or has been known  by cement engineers for a while like  titanium dioxide reduces the strength or  three calcium silicate increases  strength that's great because it really  gives us the confidence that the model  learns something meaningful  but we learned what is more important  than the model excel itself is actually  proper preprocessing so you should do  outlier filtering make sure to remove  extreme values because usually  extreme values mean it's a measurement  error but do not remove two extreme  values because they might actually teach  your machine learning uh a valuable  lesson um to figure out what's a good  threshold when to remove one that's a  good place for a hyper parameter  optimization  and also it's important to do proper  scaling our variables live on very  different scales so normalization is key  we used the robust scalar to do that but  we had to learn that the default  settings of the robust scalar are not  at robust the default settings are  scaling between the 25th and a 75th  percentile problem is if you have a data  dimension that has in the majority of  the data almost no variation but lots of  stuff is going on in the extreme ranges  using the default robust scalar would  really blow up that dimension so if you  scale between the 5th and the 95th  percentile you actually get to to our  mind a more robust robust scalar and  from now on we will use that as as a  default setting then of course we need  to do imputation  meaning because we have problems with  many missing values from experiments and  from from the measurement devices  but proper imputation also means just  throw away data that has too many  missing values because if you try to  impute these  you will end up with heavy bias in your  final machine learning pipeline  but this is already a nice step where  you can already use machine learning to  impute some columns because our columns  are highly co-linear so we might be able  to predict column a from column b and c  however still do simple imputation like  a group media and four columns that have  many missing features because uh if you  would again use machine learning here  you would introduce bias into your model  um when to do which of the three um and  defining thresholds again a good place  for a hyper parameter optimization  okay so now we have our model in place  um and we are soon to deploy the model  to actually run stuff in a cement plant  and optimize cement manufacturing  so of course before we deploy our model  we really want to know and get a good  estimate of how  well the model is going to behave in  this real-life setting so realistic  model evaluation is key  um so  in order to estimate the model  performance in the real life what you  can always do is cross validation the  problem is the assumption of  cross-validation of random regular  cross-validation is that your data is  independent  in a real-life setting especially in the  process industry like cement that is not  the case we have a constant stream of  material where we eventually take  samples so our data is highly highly  correlated in time i mean you can see  that here if i draw a high strength  sample today  then it's quite likely that tomorrow i  will also draw a high strength sample so  data is really highly correlated and the  ordering of data when it was taken  really matters so we cannot do  random cross validation because that  would yield huge data leakage so what we  do have to do instead is temporal cross  validation meaning we order our data  according to the time dimension and then  we train on a consecutive interval  followed by another consecutive interval  which we then use for validation but  even that wouldn't be enough for us  because remember the arrival of the true  labels the actual cement strength takes  28 days because i have to form a  concrete cube let it harden for 28 days  and then crush it and  this we had to bake in into the cross  validation meaning we have to skip data  for 28 days before we actually start the  validation full to really make our cross  validation resemble what's going on in  real life in real cement plans and if we  wouldn't do that and just use regular  random cross validation we would  actually overestimate mobile performance  by 30 and more and there's nothing more  embarrassing for a machine learning  engineer than figuring out later that  your model deployed in production is  much much worse from what you estimated  from your training data  okay so now we can use temporal cross  validation to measure realistic kpis  like mean squared error root mean  squared error i mean absolute error you  name it so for instance here you see  the simulation like one temporal cross  validation of one particular model over  one year prediction in red and true  labels in blue um the rule the mean  absolute error here is 1.4 megapascals  so  that then this leaves you is that good  is that bad what does 1.4 megapixels an  error actually mean so we need a way to  really figure out how our model behaves  with and compare it to something  meaningful and what you can always do is  compare for instance measures like the  mean squared error to the target  variance like the variance in the actual  target you're predicting and that's an  analog to r squared so it basically  tells you how much of the variance in my  target is actually explained by my  machine learning model  however that's a bit of difficult of a  measure because the variance of the data  we're looking at really depends on the  selected time frame so for instance if i  actually compare them or evaluate my  model only over a short amount of time  uh like this down here i only observe at  like a small variance in the target  variable because our data is highly  correlated also if i choose a larger  window i might get a larger variance in  the actual target variable and in fact  because we have a non-stationary process  it doesn't converge for infinite time  windows also we don't have infinite time  windows um so  if you evaluate models of a particular  time frame uh you have to be aware that  i can't compare model performances if i  normalize it by the target variants  over different time frames so if i  choose one year i have to stick with one  year and it doesn't make sense to  compare it with evaluations of half a  year or even shorter time scales but in  order to get rid of this  assumption or this problem of choosing a  time frame what you can always do is  compare your model performance to simple  bass lines so you should always use  simple bass lines and  a first natural choice of simple bass  line is always a linear model  still our nilia model has quite some  assumptions because we still need to do  pre-processing for the linear models so  that means we need to bake in  assumptions for scaling imputation and  of course  make a choice of features  and here's one advantage when you have  highly correlated data in time  there's a very easy way to come up with  a very simple baseline that has  basically no assumptions baked in and  that's just using a rolling median or a  rolling mean over a certain time frame  or the last x samples and then you have  a baseline that has no assumptions apart  from just the window size or the number  of samples  and if you can't even if your machine  learning even can't beat this rolling  media it's probably not worth applying  machine learning to it so that's a very  very nice baseline for settings like  these  so  this was already a lot of machining but  we quickly figured out that machine  learning is only the tip of the iceberg  um so this is our machine learning code  and this is all the rest and i mean i  took this from  hidden technical depth and machine  learning which is a great paper and i  think this picture popped up  during pi data already quite a lot but  it's it's so true right so for us about  two uh two full-time employees worked  about two months on the machine learning  code and now there's seven hats and  counting working three years and  counting on all the rest i mean for  instance there's lots of configuration  for us we have many different machine  learning models per customer we  have different machine learning models  per compressive strength for different  grouping softwares and all of this  somehow needs to be managed and then of  course there's lots of work going into  the serving infrastructure i mean our  models are dockerized and then served on  aws fargate or kubernetes and that needs  to be built and maintained and of course  there's loads of monitoring like  monitoring if just the application runs  if the machine learning models are  running if they are any good if data is  coming in if the data is any good and so  on and so forth  and here a big thank you uh to our  machine learning league nico who  actually replicated that image  from our code base in gitlab and you can  see it actually fits quite well so up  here is the model code including by the  way the feature transformations and  feature extractions and here  is all the rest yeah so way more than  the actual machine learning model  okay so i told you that there's lots of  monitoring and we do need lots of  monitoring because it turns out running  machine learning on customer provided  data is really hard  because customer data can change so for  instance there might be changes in the  data format i mean this happens why not  change a date right from european to  american or that's my favorite using  full stop and comma as decimal  separators almost unpredictably i mean  one is the english notation for decimals  the other one is the german one why not  mix both for fun  but there might actually be changes to  the data itself  so there might be humans in the loop  that are prettifying the data meaning i  mean some of the experiments like a  compressive strength experiment where  you actually have to crush the cube are  quite manual and sometimes humans tend  to if they get a measure that is uh not  in their favor slightly correct the  number uh to make it look prettier  problem is if you do that this will  really confuse machine learning so  here's like lots of advocating and  teaching involved that  that processes like this really have to  stop once you start deploying machine  learning and uh what regularly happens  is that there's a recalibrations of the  devices that provide the data frost like  the x-ray diffrectomy or x-ray  fluorescence i mean cement is a very  fine powder so stuff gets dirty pretty  quickly or gets clogged so you need to  replace parts and then recalibrate  devices and this then can cause  huge data and concept drifts and we need  to detect these and actually react upon  that and then there might actually be  problems with uh the sample sites so  where the samples are drawn  itself so as i told you there are  samples drawn right after the cement  modes these are the ones that we use to  make the predictions and actually steer  the cement mill grinding and however  there are also samples taken at the  delivery silo when the cement before the  cement gets shipped to the concrete  customers and these are usually the ones  that have compressive strength tests  attached to it so these are the ones  that have the true labels  that we can use for training and these  ones are used for prediction or  inference  and it could be that due to the physical  properties of this sample side and that  sample side they might be not completely  representative and there might be slight  shift for instance in the particle size  distribution so we have to know about  this and then bake that into our models  so there's lots of stuff that can  actually go wrong with data  so for that we actually introduced  like very sophisticated data science  processes whenever we onboard customer  and once after we have onboarded them so  for every new customer what we do is  what we call a data deep dive so that's  they send us historic data of three to  four years um and we really go through  each and every measurement of each and  every cement one by one and look at  idiosyncrasies and problems and whether  the data makes sense and if we spot  something that looks out of the ordinary  we go back to the customer and together  try to figure out what went wrong in the  plant or what happened in the plant at  that point in time that could explain  the shifts in the data  and then when we decided okay the data  looks feasible for machine learning and  it makes sense to actually apply it  then we can start integrating into the  systems of our customers and our  customers are cement plants and they are  not i.t companies meaning they have  quite some legacy systems in place and  integrating into legacy systems  is quite some work so usually that takes  about half a year  so and then once we finally have a  customer in production it doesn't stop  there of course so we need to do  continuous monitoring of  customers data so if there's data drifts  or shifts occurring or if if something  changes  other changes and then of course we need  to do continuous evaluation of all our  machine learning models  and i'm not kidding we have with each  and every customer weekly meetings where  we discuss machine learning and data  issues and changes in the plan because  as i told you if they recalibrate a  device or if they built a new mill or if  they changed a silo setting we need to  know about this  so  