agers also  not scales sublinearity with the number  of customers that you have so  with all this um we actually came to the  conclusion that in order to turn this  into a  viable business we have to hunt  elephants or hopefully whales but for  now elephants meaning these are  customers who are willing to pay about  uh a hundred thousand us dollars a year  um uh because uh otherwise it really  don't make sense to put industrial  machine learning into uh  fortunately cement customers are of that  kind uh but one challenge of course is  nobody gives away um this amount of  money lightly so uh negotiations and  sales processes might take some time so  like half a year a year or longer so you  need to be in for the long haul  and  that pretty much  concludes my talk thank you very much  for having me  here are some key takeaways from  from my slides  i'm  reachable on slack afterwards and in  gather so if you want to gather at the  cosy fireplace uh meet me up there and  if we i think we still have some time  left so i also happy to take some  questions here  are there questions in the q a  yes okay let me see  uh how do you exactly blend linear  regression in a  random forest um we use a random forest  uh correcting the errors of the linear  model works uh super nicely  um okay same question again  uh what software do you use to generate  the vis release of the ml models uh dash  plotly dash dash is great  um  could you elaborate more on there's no  mvp in industrial ml yes so the problem  is of course that um  there  is i mean loads of stuff that you  know  that you really need to implement i mean  it's not that you can come up with a  jupiter notebook um with just some  historic data and show that to your  customers and then say hey look um  there's my machine learning model  because there are there's nothing  actionable from that so in order to  really have actionable ml that changes  something in the plan  you have to be fully integrated into the  plan so like this